--- a/docs/en/dev/backend/01-nvidia-target-info.md
+++ b/docs/en/dev/backend/01-nvidia-target-info.md
@@ -364,6 +364,17 @@ For rank greater than two, canonical source reshapes the compute tensor to
 result shape. This is what makes the single row tile and flattened row-grid law
 exact rather than producer-dependent.
 
+The resolved `reduction_tile_size=128` is a contraction-tile budget, not an
+unconditional tile extent. For positive last extent `N`, the actual contraction
+tile is `r=min(128, largest_power_of_two_divisor(N))`, and lowering materializes
+`ceil(N/r)` reduction chunks. Thus `N=128` uses `r=128` with no chunk loop,
+`N=256` uses `r=128` with two chunks, and every odd `N` uses `r=1`. The schedule
+contains exactly one positive power-of-two row tile `T`, with `T<=rows`, where
+`rows=1` for rank one and otherwise `product(shape[:-1])`; the static grid is
+`(ceil(rows/T), 1, 1)`. Preparation rejects `T*r>16,777,216`, the pinned CUDA
+Tile compiler maximum tile-element count, and rejects `ceil(N/r)>INT32_MAX`
+because TensorIR lowering materializes the chunk-loop bound as i32.
+
 The ABI contains exactly ordered `Input0` and `Result0` tensor descriptors, one
 read-only input plus a `nil` result alias, two pointer arguments, static grid
 policy selected by descriptor zero, zero workspace, and the shared fixed launch
